Java & Spring Boot Interview Notes

1. Abstract Class vs Interface

→ Abstract class is used when related classes share common state and behaviour.
→ Interface is used when different classes must follow the same contract.
→ A class can extend only one class but can implement multiple interfaces.
→ Abstract classes can have constructors and instance variables. Interfaces can have abstract, default, static, and private methods.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

2. Abstract Class Example

abstract class Animal {

    // Common behaviour shared by all animals
    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Animal is eating");
    }

    // Each animal must provide its own implementation
    abstract void sound();
}

class Dog extends Animal {

    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Animal animal = new Dog();
        animal.eat();
        animal.sound();
    }
}

Output:
Animal is eating
Dog barks

→ Here, Animal provides common behaviour using eat().
→ Dog implements the abstract sound() method.
→ We cannot create an object directly using new Animal().

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

3. Interface Example

interface Payment {
    // Every payment implementation must provide this behaviour
    void pay();
}

class CreditCardPayment implements Payment {

    @Override
    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("Payment using credit card");
    }
}

class UpiPayment implements Payment {

    @Override
    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("Payment using UPI");
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Payment payment = new UpiPayment();
        payment.pay();
    }
}

Output:
Payment using UPI

→ Payment defines a contract.
→ CreditCardPayment and UpiPayment implement that contract differently.
→ We can add new payment types without changing code that depends on the Payment interface.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

4. Real-Time Project Example

→ Abstract class: Use BaseNotificationService if email, SMS, and push notifications share common fields or implementation.

→ Interface: Use NotificationService to define sendNotification(), which email, SMS, and push services implement differently.

Example:

interface NotificationService {
    void sendNotification(String message);
}

class EmailService implements NotificationService {
    public void sendNotification(String message) {
        System.out.println("Sending email: " + message);
    }
}

class SmsService implements NotificationService {
    public void sendNotification(String message) {
        System.out.println("Sending SMS: " + message);
    }
}

→ This design makes it easy to add a new notification type without changing the code that calls NotificationService.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

5. Interview Answer

→ Abstract class represents a common base with shared state or implementation.
→ Interface defines a contract that implementing classes must follow.
→ A class can extend one abstract or concrete class but implement multiple interfaces.
→ I prefer interfaces for loose coupling and abstract classes when related classes need shared code.
